Found during k250 (2026-10-31, ocp-state-worker): validate() is only called by tests, although comments claim that `ocp apply` lists its errors. At runtime only the croaking accessors guard the config (gpu_driver, gpu_amd_*), and only when someone reads them. Also, gpu.driver has no validate() check of its own.

Clarify: should `ocp apply` (and `ocp status`?) call validate() before the first host intervention and report all errors at once (STDERR, exit != 0), or do the comments go? Decide that, then wire it up, plus gpu.driver in validate(). Lanes: state (Config) + apply (call site).

Decision (main agent, my call): yes — ocp apply (and --dry-run) calls OCP::Config::validate() before anything touches a host or the cluster, prints every error at once on STDERR and exits 1; gpu.driver gets its own validate() check. ocp status does not gate on it (read side; it must still report a cluster whose ocp.yaml has a mistake). The comments that claim apply lists the errors then become true.

